 ## MVP Scope
 
@@ -67,6 +67,29 @@ ## User Administration
 
 ## Queue and Tickets
 
+### Queue lifecycle
+
+A queue belongs to exactly one entity and carries a name, a slug and an active flag. Only the entity's **owner or admin** may create or update it (`App\Actions\CreateQueue`, `App\Actions\UpdateQueue`).
+
+- The name is trimmed and must be 1–255 characters.
+- The slug defaults to `Str::slug(name)` (`"Atendimento Geral"` → `atendimento-geral`); an explicit slug is slugified too. It is unique **within the entity** — the same slug may exist in another entity.
+- New queues are active. A duplicate slug is a validation error, including when the database unique index catches a race.
+- Queues are **never hard-deleted**: deactivation (`is_active = false`) is how a queue is retired. Its tickets are preserved, and issuing or calling on it keeps failing with `InactiveQueueException`. A manager may reactivate it through the normal update path.
+
+### Per-queue access
+
+`queue_access` grants a membership permissions on one queue; the only permission today is `call`. Only the queue entity's owner or admin may manage grants (`App\Actions\SetQueueAccess`). A non-empty permission list upserts the row, while an empty list revokes (deletes) it. Unknown or non-string permissions, and a membership/queue pair from different entities, are validation errors; non-managers are forbidden. Grants and revokes write `queue.access.granted` / `queue.access.revoked` to the `audit` log channel with `actor_id`, `queue_id` and `entity_membership_id` (an empty revoke with no row logs nothing).
+
+`App\Policies\QueuePolicy` is the single source of truth for queue authorization:
+
+| Ability | Allowed |
+| ------- | ------- |
+| `create` (entity argument) | Owner or admin of that entity |
+| `update`, `manageAccess` | Owner or admin of the queue's entity |
+| `call` | Owner or admin of the queue's entity, always; an employee only with a `queue_access` row **for that queue** whose `permissions` array contains `call` (a corrupted non-array value denies) |
+
+Both call paths enforce `call`: `CallNextTicket` checks the queue is active and then authorizes the caller, and `Ticket::call()` locks the ticket, rejects inactive queues with `InactiveQueueException`, authorizes (even when the ticket was already called) and only then enforces the "waiting tickets only" `LogicException`.
+
